  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nRidging is evident over the south central CONUS on this evening's \nwater vapor imagery. Overshooting tops in western Nebraska are \nrunning -85C. The KLNX radar is lit up with severe convection that's \nheaded this way. It's tied to an upper level shortwave. Upscale \ngrowth has been observed in the western half of Nebraska. Model \nguidance has been consistent in keeping the resultant MCV chugging \ninto the eastern part of the state after midnight tonight and \nbringing a swath of damaging winds. Some gusts of 75mph+ are \npossible (5% at any given point). Large hail and a QLCS type tornado \ncan't be ruled out (2%), but flash flooding due to heavy rain is \nmuch more likely (15%+). Guidance does have the strongest winds\ntending to fizzle out before the storms hit the Missouri River. \n\nThe warm front continues to lie along the KS/NE border and \nanother LLJ is ramping up and transporting plenty of moisture \nover the cooler air in place. Our 00Z balloon showed 1.6\" of\nPWAT which is over the 90th percentile. Warm cloud depths of \n4,000 meters means the rain will be efficient. \n\nThe HREF has probability of 3\"+ of rain in three hours at \n30-50% for all areas north of the Platte River / I-680. It's \ncloser to 50-80% for 1\"+ of precip in 3 hours. Current FFG \nsuggests 2-3\" in three hours would be about all soil could \nhandle. Thankfully, the system looks progressive enough that \nthere won't be much for training / repeating rounds of rain.\nStill, I expect a ribbon of 3-5\" rainfall reports.",
